<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-9" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Green’s Theorem and Planar Vector Calculus</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we introduce Green’s Theorem, a fundamental result that connects line integrals
    around closed curves to double integrals over planar regions. This theorem reveals a deep
    relationship between circulation, flux, and the behavior of a vector field throughout a region,
    rather than just along its boundary.
  </p>

  <p>
    We begin by studying both the circulation and flux forms of Green’s Theorem and learning when
    each applies. Careful attention is given to orientation, region geometry, and the conditions under
    which the theorem can be used. These considerations help clarify not only how to apply the theorem,
    but also why it works.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter concludes by extending Green’s Theorem to more general regions, including regions
    with holes. By the end of this chapter, you should be able to recognize when Green’s Theorem
    simplifies a problem, choose the appropriate form, and interpret the result geometrically as a
    statement about flow and rotation in the plane.
  </p>
</introduction>


